{#
    Conflict resolution metadata (Python Jinja2)

    W3C SCXML Appendix D.2 — when a machine has `<parallel>` regions,
    multiple transitions may be enabled in a single microstep. The
    runtime `Engine._select_transitions` calls
    `_remove_conflicting_transitions`, which uses `get_parent` +
    `get_document_order` from the policy to compute exit sets and decide
    which transitions win. Documents without `<parallel>` (the common
    case) have at most one active leaf, so transition conflicts cannot
    arise and the runtime short-circuits before the conflict resolver
    runs.

    This include lives outside the `class` body and emits a runtime-
    visible note documenting which branch of the algorithm is active for
    the current machine. The parallel-regions data itself is already
    surfaced via `Policy.get_parallel_regions(...)` above, so we do not
    re-emit the same table here to avoid an unconsumed module
    constant ([[feedback-spec-mirror-parity]]).
#}

{% if model.has_parallel_states %}
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Conflict resolution (W3C SCXML Appendix D.2)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This machine declares `<parallel>` regions, so the runtime engine's
# `_select_transitions` collects one candidate per active leaf and
# routes them through `_remove_conflicting_transitions` before any
# state change. Region metadata is exposed via
# `Policy.get_parallel_regions(...)` above; the engine sorts those
# regions by document order on entry so the W3C trace is deterministic.
{% else %}
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Conflict resolution (W3C SCXML Appendix D.2)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This machine declares no `<parallel>` regions, so the engine's
# leaf-up `_select_from_chain` walk is unambiguous — the first match
# wins, and `_remove_conflicting_transitions` is short-circuited at
# the call site.
{% endif %}
